    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          People usually arrive at this comparison with one of two questions: which machine burns
          more for weight loss, or which one is kinder to sore knees. The honest answers are less
          dramatic than most comparisons suggest, and the deciding factor is often something
          practical — space, noise, or which one you will actually get on four times a week.
        </p>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Treadmill or Exercise Bike: The Verdict</h2>
<p>
            <strong>For weight loss, neither machine is clearly better: at the same effort the
            difference in calories is modest, and the machine you use most consistently wins.</strong>
            A treadmill tends to burn somewhat more at the same perceived effort because you carry your
            whole body weight, but a bike makes it easier to do more total work without joint
            complaints.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For sore knees, an exercise bike is usually the gentler option</strong>, because it
            removes impact entirely and keeps the knee moving through a controlled range. But walking
            on a treadmill is itself low impact, and many people with knee pain tolerate it well. Which
            suits your knees is a question for a clinician, not a review site.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On everything else, they trade places.</strong> The treadmill wins on bone loading,
            training everyday walking, and versatility for runners. The bike wins on space, noise,
            price, maintenance and low-impact comfort. If you have neighbours below or very little room,
            the bike is hard to argue with; if you want to walk or run, or bone health is a priority,
            choose the treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="impact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Impact: The Core Difference</h2>
<p>
            The fundamental mechanical difference is that cycling is non-weight-bearing and walking or
            running is weight-bearing. On a bike, the saddle carries most of your weight and the pedals
            move in a fixed circle; there is no landing, so there is no impact. On a treadmill, every
            step lands your body weight onto the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            How much that matters depends on what you do on the treadmill. Running produces ground
            reaction forces in the region of two to three times body weight with each footstrike.
            Walking produces far less — close to 1.2 times body weight — which is why walking is
            generally classed as low impact. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a>
            comparison discusses those figures in more depth.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the comparison is really bike versus treadmill walking, or bike versus treadmill
            running. Against running, the bike removes a great deal of impact. Against walking, the gap
            is much smaller, and the case for the bike rests on comfort and specific conditions rather
            than on impact alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Calorie Burn at Matched Effort</h2>
<p>
            This is the most searched part of the comparison and the most misunderstood, because the
            answer depends far more on effort than on the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>At the same perceived effort, a treadmill often burns somewhat more.</strong> When
            you walk or run you support and move your whole body weight, and more muscle is involved in
            stabilising you. On a bike the saddle supports you and the work is concentrated in the legs,
            so many people feel they are working hard on a bike at a lower overall energy cost — the
            thighs fatigue before the heart and lungs are fully taxed. Trained cyclists close that gap
            considerably.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>At the same heart rate, the difference shrinks.</strong> Energy use tracks closely
            with how hard your cardiovascular system is working. If you hold the same heart rate for the
            same time on each machine, the totals tend to be broadly comparable for most people.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ignore the console figure on both.</strong> Neither machine knows your body
            composition or fitness, and both displays tend to be optimistic. A heart rate monitor is a
            better guide to effort than either screen; our article on
            <a href="/are-treadmill-calories-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether treadmill calories are accurate</a>
            explains why the numbers drift.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-loss" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Which Is Better for Weight Loss?</h2>
<p>
            Here the honest answer is that the machine is not the main lever. Exercise is excellent for
            health, fitness and maintaining a weight you have reached, but changes in body weight are
            driven largely by what and how much you eat. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-workout-for-weight-loss/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight-loss workout guide</a>
            is candid about that, and our piece on
            <a href="/does-treadmill-burn-belly-fat/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether a treadmill burns belly fat</a>
            covers why you cannot target where fat comes off.
          </p>
          <p>
            Within that, three things decide which machine serves you better. <strong>Consistency
            beats everything</strong>: the machine you use four times a week outperforms the one you use
            once, whatever the per-session figures say. <strong>Total work matters more than
            intensity</strong>: if the bike lets you exercise for longer or more often without your
            joints complaining, it may produce more total calories over a month even if the per-minute
            figure is lower. <strong>Convenience is underrated</strong>: a bike can be ridden while
            watching television or reading with very little thought, which keeps some people going far
            longer than they would on a belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are heavier and starting out, a bike can be a comfortable way in, and a walking
            treadmill is equally reasonable. Choose the one you find tolerable, and be patient.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="knees" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Which Is Better for Your Knees?</h2>
<p>
            For many people with knee pain, an exercise bike is the more comfortable machine. It removes
            impact, keeps the knee moving through a controlled range, and lets you set resistance low
            enough to keep the joint working without strain. This is why bikes are a staple of
            rehabilitation settings.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two qualifications matter. First, <strong>bike setup decides knee comfort</strong>. A saddle
            set too low forces the knee through a deep bend under load, which many people find
            aggravating; a saddle too high makes you rock and overreach. Fore-aft saddle position and
            handlebar reach matter too. A bike that hurts your knees is often a bike that is set up
            wrongly, and the manufacturer's sizing guidance or a knowledgeable fitter can help.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, <strong>walking is not the enemy</strong>. Our guide to whether
            <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are bad for your knees</a>
            sets out the evidence, which is more reassuring than many people expect, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for bad knees</a>
            roundup covers the features that help. Steep downhill and hard running are the treadmill
            uses most likely to bother sore knees; flat or gently inclined walking is often tolerated
            well. If you have a diagnosed knee condition, a physiotherapist can tell you which suits
            you — that is not something we can judge from here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Bone Loading: The Treadmill's Hidden Advantage</h2>
<p>
            Bone adapts to the loads placed on it, and it responds most to weight-bearing activity and
            impact. That is why weight-bearing exercise is routinely recommended for maintaining bone
            density, particularly for older adults and post-menopausal women.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cycling is excellent cardiovascular exercise, but because the saddle carries your weight it
            provides little of that bone-loading stimulus. Walking and running on a treadmill provide
            more of it, and running provides more again.
          </p>
          <p>
            This does not make the bike a poor choice. For someone who cannot tolerate weight-bearing
            exercise, the bike is far better than nothing. But if bone health is a specific concern —
            and for many people over 50 it reasonably is — it belongs in the decision, and many people
            who ride a bike for cardio add walking or strength training for exactly this reason. Ask a
            clinician what suits you if you have, or are at risk of, low bone density.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specificity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Muscles, Movement and Training Something You Use</h2>
<p>
            A bike works the quadriceps hard, with the glutes, hamstrings and calves contributing. It
            builds leg endurance and cardiovascular fitness effectively. What it does not train is
            walking itself — the balance, gait and tissue tolerance you use every day.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill trains a movement you perform constantly outside the gym. For older adults,
            maintaining a confident walking gait is one of the most valuable things exercise can
            preserve; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-seniors/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for seniors</a>
            guide is built around that. For runners and anyone training for a walking or running event,
            the specificity is decisive: cycling maintains fitness, but it does not prepare the legs for
            the pounding of running.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline is the treadmill's other strength. Walking uphill raises effort steeply without
            running and shifts work toward the glutes and calves; our guide to
            <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk on</a>
            covers how to use it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Space, Noise and Neighbours</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Space favours the bike decisively.</strong> An upright or spin-style bike occupies a
            small fraction of a treadmill's floor area, and it needs no safety zone behind it. A
            treadmill needs its full footprint plus roughly 6 feet of clearance behind the deck, as our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">space requirements guide</a>
            sets out. Recumbent bikes are longer than uprights but still far more compact than a
            treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise favours the bike too.</strong> Most home bikes use magnetic resistance and are
            very quiet; what noise there is stays mostly in the room. Treadmill noise that reaches a
            neighbour below is largely footfall impact transmitted through the floor, which is hard to
            fully control. If you live above someone, this factor can reasonably outrank all the others.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Power</strong> is a minor point: many bikes need only a small supply for the console,
            and some need none, while a treadmill runs a drive motor. The running cost of either is
            small.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Cost and Maintenance</h2>
<p>
            A competent exercise bike costs considerably less than a treadmill of similar build quality,
            and it is a simpler machine to own. There is no belt to lubricate, tension or centre, no
            drive motor working under load and no controller board handling large currents. Routine care
            is mostly keeping it clean and checking that bolts and pedals are tight.
          </p>
          <p>
            A treadmill asks more. Its deck needs periodic lubrication, its belt tension and tracking need
            checking, and its motor and controller board are the expensive components if something goes
            wrong. None of that is onerous — our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            takes well under an hour a year — but it is more than a bike.
          </p>
          <p>
            Both categories include connected models with screens and subscriptions. Check what a machine
            does without a membership before buying, because the ongoing fee can overtake the price
            difference between the machines within a few years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="injury" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Injury and Safety Considerations</h2>
<p>
            Each machine has its own characteristic risks, and none is high when the machine is used
            sensibly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On a treadmill</strong>, the main acute risk is a fall — misjudging a step on or off,
            losing concentration, or a stumble at speed. Clip the safety key on every session, start the
            belt before stepping onto it at a slow speed, and keep children and pets well away. Running
            brings the familiar overuse issues — shins, Achilles, knees — mostly from doing too much too
            soon. Our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-use-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to use a treadmill</a>
            covers the safe basics.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>On a bike</strong>, falls are rare. The common complaints are saddle discomfort, knee
            irritation from poor setup, and lower-back or neck stiffness from a cramped or stretched
            riding position. A recumbent bike, with a backrest and a more reclined position, is often more
            comfortable for people with back problems or balance concerns, and easier to get on and off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Which</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Choose a treadmill if:</strong> you want to walk or run; you are training for an
            event on foot; bone density is a concern; you want incline as a way to raise effort without
            speed; or maintaining your walking is a priority. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-beginners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">beginner treadmill picks</a>
            are a good starting point.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Choose an exercise bike if:</strong> knee or hip pain limits weight-bearing exercise
            and a clinician agrees cycling suits you; you have little space; you live above a neighbour;
            you want the lowest cost and maintenance; or you simply enjoy it more — which is a better
            reason than it sounds.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider both, or neither, if:</strong> you can only fit one machine and want variety,
            in which case an elliptical sits between them — weight-bearing without the impact.
          </p>
          <p>
            If at all possible, try both for twenty minutes before you buy. How each feels on your joints,
            and which you can imagine using on a dark Tuesday in February, tells you more than any
            calorie comparison.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
