          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a treadmill or exercise bike better for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is clearly better. At the same perceived effort a treadmill often burns somewhat more because you carry your full body weight, but at the same heart rate the gap narrows, and the machine you use most consistently wins. Diet drives weight change far more than the choice of machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an exercise bike better than a treadmill for bad knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, yes, because cycling removes impact and keeps the knee moving through a controlled range. But walking on a treadmill is itself low impact and many people with knee pain tolerate it well. Bike setup matters — a saddle that is too low can aggravate knees. Ask a physiotherapist what suits your knees.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a treadmill burn more calories than a bike?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the same perceived effort, usually somewhat more, because walking and running are weight-bearing and involve more muscle. At the same heart rate for the same time, totals tend to be broadly comparable. Both consoles' calorie figures are estimates and tend to run optimistic.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is cycling or walking better for belly fat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither can target belly fat; the body does not lose fat from the area being exercised. Both contribute to overall energy use, and fat loss over time comes mainly from diet combined with consistent activity. Choose the one you will keep doing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a stationary bike good for bone density?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Less so than walking or running. Cycling is non-weight-bearing because the saddle carries your weight, so it provides little of the loading bone responds to. If bone density is a concern, weight-bearing activity such as walking, plus strength training, is generally recommended; ask a clinician what suits you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which takes up less space, a treadmill or an exercise bike?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                An exercise bike, by a wide margin. An upright bike uses a small fraction of a treadmill's floor area and needs no safety clearance behind it, whereas a treadmill needs its full footprint plus roughly 6 feet of clear floor behind the deck. Bikes are also much quieter for neighbours below.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Choosing Between a Treadmill and a Bike</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most regrets in this comparison come from optimising the wrong thing. These are the four we see most.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on calories per minute</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The per-minute difference at matched effort is modest, and console figures are optimistic on both machines. What decides results over months is how often you use the machine, so choose the one you find tolerable enough to use four times a week.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a treadmill means running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Walking produces close to 1.2 times body weight per step against two to three times for running. If you plan to walk, the impact argument for the bike is much weaker than it first appears, and incline walking adds effort without speed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Riding a badly set-up bike</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A saddle that is too low forces the knee through a deep bend under load, and a cramped or stretched position strains the back and neck. Follow the manufacturer's sizing guidance, and adjust before deciding the bike disagrees with you.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting bone health</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Cycling is great for the heart and lungs but provides little bone loading. If bone density matters to you, it belongs in the decision — and if you choose the bike, consider adding walking or strength work.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

